INT. HOTEL BELMONT-CRANE - PNEUMATIC TUBE ROOM - NIGHT

A windowless room behind the front desk. Brass tubes snake up the walls to every floor, thumping quietly with canisters in transit. A sorting table. A single green-shaded lamp. A black candlestick telephone on a stool.

DETECTIVE ISIDORE QUAYLE, 50s, rumpled overcoat, hat in hand, studies a brass canister. CONCIERGE THEO LAMBERT, 30s, tailcoat, silver hair too early, stands very straight at the table. MRS. GLADYS BRANDYWINE, 60s, hotel owner, pearls, black dress, sits on a wooden chair with her handbag in her lap.

QUAYLE
Mr. Lambert, how long have you operated the tubes?

LAMBERT
Eleven years, sir.

QUAYLE
And this evening you sent a canister to room 614.

LAMBERT
At eleven oh two. A message from the front desk.

QUAYLE
What was the message?

LAMBERT
I didn't read it.

MRS. BRANDYWINE
He never reads them, Detective.

QUAYLE
Madam, I'm asking Mr. Lambert.

MRS. BRANDYWINE
And I am telling you that he is a professional.

LAMBERT
Thank you, Mrs. Brandywine.

QUAYLE
Who gave you the message?

LAMBERT
A guest. A lady in grey.

MRS. BRANDYWINE
Gladys.

QUAYLE
I beg your pardon?

MRS. BRANDYWINE
My name is Gladys. I wore grey tonight.

QUAYLE
(slowly)
Madam. Are you telling me you wrote the message?

MRS. BRANDYWINE
I am telling you nothing, Detective. I am correcting Mr. Lambert's description.

LAMBERT
Mrs. Brandywine, I never said it was you.

MRS. BRANDYWINE
No, Theo. You didn't.

The telephone rings. Everyone stops. Quayle lifts the receiver.

QUAYLE
Quayle.

OPERATOR (O.S.)
(over the line)
Detective, I have a call from Room 614.

QUAYLE
Put it through.

OPERATOR (O.S.)
Sir, it's already connected. Sir, I think the line is open.

QUAYLE
Is anyone speaking?

OPERATOR (O.S.)
No sir. I hear a clock.

QUAYLE
A clock?

OPERATOR (O.S.)
Ticking. Very loud.

Quayle holds the receiver away from his ear. All four hear it faintly, a metronome in the dark: tick, tick, tick.

LAMBERT
The grandfather clock in the penthouse hall.

MRS. BRANDYWINE
That clock stopped in 1929.

QUAYLE
Then who wound it?

Neither answers. A canister thumps in the tube. A second, a third. They roll into the sorting tray, one after another.

LAMBERT
These aren't mine.

MRS. BRANDYWINE
Open them.

QUAYLE
Madam!

MRS. BRANDYWINE
Open them, Detective, or I will.

Quayle snaps the first one open. Pulls out a slip of cream paper. Reads it. His face goes blank.

LAMBERT
What does it say?

QUAYLE
"He's behind the wall."

Silence. The tubes thump. The clock ticks on the telephone. Mrs. Brandywine stands, smooths her dress.

MRS. BRANDYWINE
I told you not to open them.

OPERATOR (O.S.)
(over the line, faint)
Sir? Sir? The ticking has stopped.

The lamp flickers, then goes out. In the dark, the tubes all begin to thump together.
